    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1886 Hoyt moved to sit beside the Rock Island Railroad, boosting cattle and hay shipping.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hoyt_kansas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1890s Hoyt built a barbed‑wire fence around the town, with gates at each corner to control livestock.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hoyt_kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hoyt survived fires in 1895 and 1909, plus tornadoes in 1916, 1917, and 1984, with little injury.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hoyt_kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hoyt’s oldest standing building is a one‑room jail built around 1900, preserved as a historic landmark.",
